  "changes": [
    {
      "asset": "core:Extras/Matrix_Updates",
      "baseline": "fail",
      "result": "pass",
      "note": "G-L4 (a)'s read-only matrix pointers — the pre-brief's expected flip. The asset only READS matrices (`/nodes/{nodeRef}/matrix`, `/nodes/3/matrix`, `/nodes/4/globalMatrix`, `/nodes/5/globalMatrix`; every matrix node in it is a `pointer/get`) and writes translations, then asserts that a matrix reflects the translation it just wrote. The p6 run had no matrix pointer registered at all (they are G-L4 (a)'s, added by this round) and its fake world had no `matrix`/`globalMatrix` to read, so every read came back invalid and its five sub-checks logged `Test Failed`. The round registers both pointers read-only, and its harness both seeds the fake world from the asset's own nodes and recomputes each matrix from the world's CURRENT values, so a write the graph made is visible in the matrix it reads next."
    },
    {
      "asset": "core:flow/setDelay_and_cancelDelay",
      "baseline": "pass",
      "result": "no result",
      "note": "FINDING — a pass that no longer reproduces; ACCEPTED under R-L5 (a) (operator, round 3) and recorded here. One-line reason: the graph exceeds G7's 16 pending delays and reads the engine's `/extensions/KHR_interactivity/delays/<n>` path, which the adapter's delay subclass does not register — both are G7/G3 decisions. In full: this asset holds more pending delays than G7 allows: 16 are live from tick 1 (measured on the adapter's own table, `engine.delays.size`, which never drops below 16 before tick 13), and the graph has 23 `flow/setDelay` nodes, so at least one of them takes `err`. The one it loses is the final 2.5 s delay whose `done` flow is the graph's ONLY test verdict (nodes 239–243), so the asset reports nothing at all under the round's adapter. Two independent changes are behind it: (1) G7's 16-pending-delay cap, which the shipped adapter did not have; (2) with the cap raised to 64 the asset reaches its verdict and reports `test/onFailed` — its `lastDelayref isValid` check fails, because that check reads a delay's `lastDelay` through `pointer/get` on `/extensions/KHR_interactivity/delays/<n>` and the round's delay subclasses schedule with the injected timers instead of the engine's `pushScheduledDelay`, which is what registers that trie path (`packages/gltf-interactivity-engine/build/index.js` L8358). The `p6` baseline passed because the shipped adapter's `SetDelay` ran the engine's own real-timer path. **Accepted under R-L5 (a): the result stays `no result`, the fixture stays, and nothing in the round changes for it.**"
    },
    {
      "asset": "math:math/matDecomp-Shear",
      "baseline": "fail",
      "result": "pass",
      "note": "G-L4 (a)'s read-only matrix pointers. The asset reads `/nodes/[nodeIndex]/globalMatrix` and writes the decomposed translation. The p6 run had no matrix pointer registered at all — the pointers are G-L4 (a)'s, added by this round — and its fake world had no `matrix`/`globalMatrix` to read, so a read of one could not resolve. The round's harness seeds the fake world from the asset's own nodes (a node's local matrix, and `globalMatrix` composed through its parents), so the read resolves and the asset's own assertions pass, because the harness also recomputes a node's matrix from the world's CURRENT values. The pre-brief's other expected flip, `core:Extras/Matrix_Updates`, flips too (see its own note)."
    }
  ],
